export const DATA_47: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-055", num: 55, title: "Đề 055", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Impact of Light Pollution", paragraphs: ["When most people think about pollution, they imagine smog hanging over a city, plastic waste floating in the ocean, or chemicals seeping into rivers. Few people consider light to be a form of pollution. Yet in recent decades, scientists and environmental groups have begun drawing attention to a problem that affects billions of people and countless animals worldwide: light pollution. This refers to the excessive and misdirected artificial light produced by cities, roads, factories, and homes that brightens the night sky and disrupts natural darkness.", "One of the most visible effects of light pollution is the disappearance of the night sky for people living in or near cities. A century ago, anyone standing outdoors on a clear night could look up and see thousands of stars, including the faint band of the Milky Way stretching across the sky. Today, according to researchers, nearly 80 percent of people in North America and Europe live under skies so bright that they can no longer see the Milky Way at all. This loss is not merely an aesthetic one. Astronomers and scientists who study the universe rely on dark skies to observe distant stars and galaxies, and light pollution is increasingly forcing observatories to relocate to remote mountain locations far from urban centres.", "The effects of artificial light on wildlife are equally serious. Many animals depend on natural patterns of light and darkness to regulate their behaviour, feeding, and reproduction. Sea turtle hatchlings, for example, instinctively move toward the brightest horizon after emerging from their nests on dark beaches — a behaviour that evolved over millions of years to guide them toward the sea, which naturally reflects moonlight. When artificial lights from beachside hotels and roads outshine the ocean's natural glow, large numbers of hatchlings become disoriented and crawl inland instead, where they die from dehydration or predators. Similar disruptions affect migrating birds, which use stars for navigation and can become fatally confused by the lit windows of tall buildings.", "Humans, too, are affected by light pollution in ways that are only recently becoming clear. The human body relies on a natural cycle of light and darkness to produce melatonin , a hormone that regulates sleep. When people are exposed to bright artificial light at night — whether from street lights outside their bedroom windows or from the screens of their phones and laptops — melatonin production is suppressed , leading to poorer sleep quality. Researchers have linked chronic sleep disruption to a range of health problems including weakened immune function, increased risk of cardiovascular disease, and higher rates of anxiety and depression.", "There is also a significant economic dimension to light pollution. Much of the artificial light produced at night is wasted — shining upward into the sky or sideways rather than downward onto the ground where it is actually needed. The International Dark-Sky Association estimates that about one-third of all outdoor lighting in the United States alone is wasted in this way, costing billions of dollars every year in unnecessary energy consumption. Using better-designed, downward-facing light fixtures and switching to more energy-efficient technologies like LED lighting can significantly reduce both wasted light and electricity costs.", "Encouragingly, awareness of light pollution is growing, and a number of communities around the world have taken meaningful steps to address it. Several towns in the United Kingdom, rural areas of New Zealand, and parts of southern Spain have been officially designated as \"Dark Sky Reserves\" — protected areas where strict limits on artificial lighting help preserve natural darkness for both wildlife and stargazers. Astronomers, conservationists, and local governments are increasingly working together to develop lighting policies that balance the need for public safety with the right of all living things to experience a naturally dark night.", "Light pollution may not produce the dramatic images associated with other environmental problems, but its effects on human health, wildlife survival, scientific research, and energy consumption are real and growing. Addressing it requires nothing more than thoughtful changes to the way we use artificial light — changes that are already within our reach."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Cities must completely eliminate artificial lighting at night to solve the problem of light pollution.", "The disappearance of visible stars is the most serious consequence of urban light pollution.", "Animals are more severely affected by light pollution than humans because they cannot adapt to artificial light.", "Light pollution is a growing environmental problem that harms human health, wildlife, scientific research, and wastes energy."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what has happened to the ability of most people in North America and Europe to see the night sky?", options: ["Air pollution from factories has made the night sky too cloudy for stargazing.", "Nearly 80 percent of people now live under skies too bright to see the Milky Way.", "Improvements in telescope technology have replaced the need to see stars with the naked eye.", "The Milky Way has gradually shifted position and is no longer visible from the Northern Hemisphere."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"misdirected\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["turned off or blocked by physical barriers", "produced deliberately to cause harm", "extremely powerful and difficult to control", "aimed or sent in the wrong direction"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do sea turtle hatchlings naturally move toward the brightest horizon?", options: ["An instinct that developed over millions of years guides them toward the ocean's reflected light.", "Researchers have trained them to associate bright light with access to food and water.", "They rely on the brightness of the horizon to identify safe areas free from predators.", "They are attracted to the warmth of artificial lights near hotels and roads."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 3 (\"a behaviour that evolved... to guide them toward the sea\") refers to _______.", options: ["beachside hotels", "dark beaches", "sea turtle hatchlings", "migrating birds"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one health problem that researchers have linked to chronic sleep disruption caused by artificial light?", options: ["Weakened immune function and increased risk of cardiovascular disease.", "Higher likelihood of experiencing headaches and migraines.", "Increased risk of developing vision problems and eye strain.", "Greater chance of developing food allergies and digestive disorders."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"suppressed\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["transferred from one part of the body to another", "reduced or blocked from functioning normally", "released in larger quantities than usual", "gradually increased to an unhealthy level"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the International Dark-Sky Association say about wasted outdoor lighting in the United States?", options: ["Most outdoor lights are turned on unnecessarily during the daytime.", "The United States wastes more energy on indoor lighting than on outdoor street lighting.", "Around one-third of all outdoor lighting shines upward or sideways rather than downward, wasting energy.", "Light pollution in the United States has decreased by one-third thanks to LED technology."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a health consequence of disrupted sleep caused by light pollution?", options: ["Decline in vision and increased eye sensitivity.", "Weakened immune function.", "Increased risk of cardiovascular disease.", "Higher rates of anxiety and depression."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the final two paragraphs about solving the problem of light pollution?", options: ["Meaningful progress is possible through practical changes in lighting design and coordinated community efforts.", "Only wealthy nations with advanced technology can afford to take effective action against light pollution.", "Solving light pollution will require cities to shut down all artificial lighting permanently.", "Light pollution can only be addressed after all other forms of environmental pollution have been resolved."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Legend of the Machine Wolf", paragraphs: ["In the forests of northern Finland, a peculiar tradition has survived for over a century alongside the nation's rapid industrialisation. Known locally as \"Konekusi\" — loosely translated as the Machine Wolf — the legend describes a creature that is neither fully animal nor fully machine, but something in between: a wolf-shaped entity said to roam abandoned factories, old railway yards, and rusting mines at night. Unlike many folklore monsters designed to terrify, the Machine Wolf occupies a morally ambiguous position in the stories told about it. In some accounts it destroys what has been left carelessly to decay; in others it guards the forgotten places where human labour once thrived.", "The origins of the legend can be traced to the late nineteenth century, when Finland was undergoing rapid industrial expansion under Russian rule. New factories, sawmills, and iron mines transformed the landscape of previously wild regions almost overnight. Workers who moved from rural villages to these industrial sites often experienced a profound sense of dislocation — cut off from their ancestral connection to forests and nature while being absorbed into the mechanical rhythms of shift work and production quotas. Folklorists believe that the Machine Wolf emerged from this cultural tension as a symbolic figure: a creature that embodied both the wildness of the old world and the grinding power of the new.", "The most widely circulated version of the legend describes the Machine Wolf as enormous — twice the size of a natural wolf — with fur said to resemble rusted iron filaments and eyes that glow with a dull orange light, like the embers of a dying furnace. It moves without sound despite its mechanical bulk, and workers who claimed to have glimpsed it reported a strong smell of oil and burnt metal in the air. Those who encountered it and fled unharmed were said to have been judged as honest labourers; those who disappeared were described in hushed tones as having been \"reclaimed\" — taken back by the industrial world they had exploited or abandoned.", "Scholars who study oral traditions have found that the Machine Wolf narrative shares structural similarities with legends from other rapidly industrialising societies. In Welsh coal mining communities of the nineteenth century, similar stories described underground creatures guarding collapsed tunnels. In the industrial regions of the Ruhr Valley in Germany, legends of iron giants that walked at night were common among factory workers well into the early twentieth century. These parallels suggest that the Machine Wolf is not simply a Finnish curiosity but part of a broader human tendency to process the psychological upheaval of industrialisation through the language of the supernatural.", "What makes the Finnish legend particularly interesting to contemporary researchers is how it has adapted over time. Rather than fading as industrial sites were demolished or reclaimed by nature, it has evolved to incorporate new settings and anxieties. By the 1980s, accounts began placing the creature in decommissioned nuclear facilities and abandoned Cold War military installations. In the early 2000s, stories emerged of the Machine Wolf appearing in server farms and data centres — places associated with invisible but immense technological power. Each adaptation, folklorists argue, reflects a generational need to give shape and moral weight to technologies that feel overwhelming and beyond individual control.", "Today, the Machine Wolf has achieved something of a cultural renaissance in Finland. It appears on murals in the city of Tampere — once a major industrial hub — and has been adopted as a symbol by several environmental organisations that campaign for the responsible decommissioning of industrial sites. A short animated film produced by a Helsinki studio in 2019 introduced the legend to international audiences and sparked academic interest in comparable legends from Japan, Brazil, and India. Whether experienced as a childhood story whispered around a fire, a piece of street art, or an object of scholarly study, the Machine Wolf endures because it speaks to something universal: the human need to make sense of the powerful, impersonal forces that shape the world we live in.", "The longevity of the legend is itself a form of testimony. In an age when digital technologies are transforming labour, identity, and community as dramatically as the factories of the nineteenth century once did, the Machine Wolf may be more relevant now than at any point in its strange history. [1] Legends like this remind us that the anxiety produced by rapid technological change is not new — it is, in fact, one of the oldest human stories. [2] And perhaps that recognition, more than any practical solution, is what stories like the Machine Wolf have always been designed to offer. [3] In giving form to what frightens us, folklore does not eliminate fear — it makes it [4] bearable."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["The origins, evolution, and cultural significance of a Finnish legend that reflects human responses to industrialisation and technological change.", "How Finland's environmental organisations have used ancient mythology to promote industrial safety regulations.", "A comparison of industrial folklore traditions across Europe and their influence on modern horror fiction.", "Evidence that the Machine Wolf was a real animal encountered by workers in nineteenth-century Finnish factories."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do folklorists believe the Machine Wolf legend emerged in the late nineteenth century?", options: ["It arose from the cultural tension workers felt between their connection to nature and the mechanical demands of industrial life.", "The legend was imported from Russian folklore and adapted by Finnish workers during the imperial period.", "Workers created the legend to explain mysterious accidents that occurred in poorly maintained factories.", "Factory owners invented the legend to discourage workers from leaving their jobs at night."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"embers\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["glowing remains of a slowly dying fire", "small pieces of wood used to start a fire", "thick clouds of smoke rising from a furnace", "sharp metallic fragments scattered on the ground"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do the parallel legends from Wales and the Ruhr Valley suggest about the Machine Wolf?", options: ["Finnish workers must have travelled to Wales and Germany and brought back similar stories.", "It is part of a wider pattern in which societies undergoing industrialisation use supernatural stories to cope with psychological upheaval.", "The Machine Wolf legend was invented by European scholars seeking to find connections between national folklores.", "Industrial legends like the Machine Wolf were deliberately created by governments to maintain worker discipline."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 5 (\"Rather than fading... it has evolved to incorporate new settings\") refers to _______.", options: ["the anxiety felt by modern technology workers", "the Machine Wolf legend", "the process of decommissioning old factories", "the industrial landscape of northern Finland"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, in which locations did stories of the Machine Wolf begin to appear from the early 2000s onwards?", options: ["Underground railway systems and urban construction sites.", "Offshore oil platforms and deep-sea mining operations.", "Remote research stations and government surveillance facilities.", "Server farms and data centres associated with invisible technological power."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"process\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["to deal with and make sense of a difficult experience", "to manufacture or produce a physical item in a factory", "to transmit data through an electronic network", "to record information in an official document"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is an example of the Machine Wolf's \"cultural renaissance\" in Finland?", options: ["A Helsinki animation studio released a short film about the legend in 2019 that reached international audiences.", "Environmental organisations in Helsinki voted to ban all industrial activity near wolf habitats.", "The Finnish government declared the Machine Wolf an official national symbol in 2015.", "Finnish schools introduced the Machine Wolf legend as a compulsory part of the national history curriculum."], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'New generations of storytellers, game designers, and filmmakers have begun drawing on it as a rich source of imagery for exploring humanity's relationship with artificial intelligence.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a way the Machine Wolf appears in contemporary Finnish culture?", options: ["Used as a symbol by environmental organisations.", "Depicted in murals in the city of Tampere.", "Adopted as the official mascot of a Finnish national sports team.", "Featured in a short animated film produced by a Helsinki studio."], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Rise of Job Hopping in the 21st Century", paragraphs: ["For much of the twentieth century, career loyalty was considered both a professional virtue and a practical strategy. Workers who remained with a single employer for decades were rewarded with pensions, promotions, and a sense of institutional belonging. Changing jobs frequently — a practice that has come to be known as \"job hopping\" — was widely viewed as a sign of unreliability or poor judgement, and candidates with fragmented employment histories often struggled to secure serious consideration from hiring managers. That consensus, however, has shifted dramatically over the past two decades, and job hopping has been reframed in much of the professional world as a mark of adaptability and ambition rather than instability.", "Several structural changes in the global economy have contributed to this shift. The decline of lifetime employment guarantees in both the private and public sectors has meant that workers can no longer assume that loyalty will be reciprocated by employers. High-profile corporate downsizings, offshoring, and the rise of short-term contract work have demonstrated repeatedly that companies prioritise profitability over employee tenure. In this environment, workers — particularly those in knowledge industries such as technology, finance, and media — have rationally concluded that their own career advancement is best served by moving between organisations rather than waiting for internal progression that may never materialise.", "Research consistently supports the financial case for job hopping. Studies conducted by compensation analysts in the United States have found that employees who stay with the same employer for more than two years earn, on average, around 50 percent less over their careers than those who change jobs regularly. The mechanism is straightforward: internal salary increases are typically capped by budget cycles and pay band structures, while external job offers are unconstrained by such limits and frequently reflect a candidate's full market value. In competitive sectors, the gap between what a loyal long-term employee earns and what a newly hired external candidate commands for the same role can be striking — a phenomenon sometimes called the \"loyalty penalty\" .", "Beyond financial gain, job hoppers often cite professional development as a central motivation. Moving between organisations exposes workers to a wider range of working cultures, management styles, technologies, and problem-solving approaches than most single employers can offer. A software engineer who has worked across startups, mid-sized firms, and large corporations, for example, develops a breadth of technical and interpersonal experience that is increasingly valued in an economy where versatility is prized above specialisation in a single context. Recruiters in several industries have noted a growing preference for candidates who demonstrate the capacity to learn and contribute quickly in unfamiliar environments — a quality that job hopping, by its nature, tends to cultivate.", "Despite these arguments, concerns about job hopping persist among employers and organisational psychologists. Critics argue that very frequent movement — changing roles every six to twelve months — can signal a difficulty with commitment or an inability to navigate complex, long-term projects that take years to reach fruition. There is also a legitimate concern about institutional knowledge: when experienced employees depart frequently, organisations lose the accumulated understanding of internal systems, client relationships, and operational nuance that cannot be easily codified or transferred. Some researchers have also challenged the assumption that breadth of experience necessarily translates into depth of competence, noting that genuine mastery of a discipline typically requires sustained engagement over years rather than episodic exposure across many contexts.", "The generational dimension of this trend is significant. Surveys of working attitudes consistently show that Millennials and Generation Z employees place considerably higher value on career growth, workplace flexibility, and meaningful work than their predecessors, and are substantially less willing to tolerate stagnant roles or rigid organisational cultures in exchange for job security. These generational expectations have reshaped employer behaviour in visible ways: companies that once offered modest annual raises now compete with sign-on bonuses, remote work options, accelerated promotion timelines, and investment in professional development programmes, all in an effort to retain talent that is increasingly aware of its own market value and unwilling to accept less.", "Ultimately, the rise of job hopping reflects a broader recalibration of the relationship between worker and employer — one in which loyalty is no longer automatically assumed on either side, and in which the terms of engagement are renegotiated with each career move. Whether this shift represents a healthier, more honest labour market or an erosion of the mutual commitment that once produced stable institutions and long-term innovation remains a question that economists, sociologists, and human resource professionals continue to debate with considerable vigour ."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Job hopping is universally accepted as the most effective career strategy in all modern industries.", "Employers are solely responsible for the increase in job hopping because they failed to reward employee loyalty.", "Research proves that job hopping always leads to higher salaries and greater professional satisfaction.", "The rise of job hopping reflects significant economic and generational changes, offering both advantages and legitimate concerns."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what has led workers to conclude that moving between organisations is better for their careers?", options: ["Corporate downsizings, offshoring, and the growth of short-term contracts have shown that employer loyalty is not guaranteed.", "Government policies have made it legally easier to change employers without financial penalty.", "Most companies now actively encourage employees to gain experience at rival firms before returning.", "Educational institutions began advising graduates to prioritise salary over job stability from the late 1990s onwards."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"versatility\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the willingness to accept lower pay in exchange for greater job security", "the ability to adapt and perform effectively across a variety of different situations", "loyalty to a single employer demonstrated over an extended period of time", "deep technical knowledge gained through years of study in one field"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the term \"loyalty penalty\" refer to?", options: ["The reputational damage suffered by workers who leave organisations after fewer than two years.", "A reduction in pension benefits for employees who change jobs more than three times in a decade.", "The gap in earnings between long-term employees and newly hired external candidates performing the same role.", "A financial fine imposed on workers who leave their employer before completing a minimum period of service."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"those\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["employers who offer competitive external salaries", "compensation analysts conducting salary research", "workers who remain with the same company for over a decade", "employees who change jobs on a regular basis"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what concern do critics raise about employees who change jobs frequently?", options: ["Their departures cause organisations to lose accumulated knowledge of internal systems and client relationships.", "They often fail to develop meaningful professional networks because they move too quickly between companies.", "They are less likely to complete advanced academic qualifications while employed.", "They tend to demand unrealistically high salaries that distort industry-wide pay scales."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"vigour\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["growing public indifference", "considerable energy and intensity", "formal academic rigour and precision", "mild disagreement and uncertainty"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how have companies responded to the increased willingness of younger workers to change jobs?", options: ["They have introduced strict contractual penalties for employees who resign within the first two years.", "They have lobbied governments to restrict the frequency with which workers can legally change employers.", "They have begun offering benefits such as sign-on bonuses, remote work, and accelerated promotion to retain talent.", "They have restructured annual performance reviews to make internal promotions faster and more transparent."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a benefit of moving between organisations for professional development?", options: ["Exposure to a wider range of working cultures and management styles.", "Experience with different technologies and problem-solving approaches.", "Development of the ability to learn and contribute quickly in unfamiliar environments.", "Opportunities to build long-lasting personal friendships with colleagues across industries."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This stands in sharp contrast to the previous generation's expectation that a single company would provide both financial security and a defined career path from entry level to retirement.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Era of AI Slop: Quality vs. Quantity in the Digital Age", paragraphs: ["A new term has quietly entered the vocabulary of digital culture: \"AI slop.\" Coined by critics and circulated widely in technology journalism and online commentary from around 2023 onwards, the phrase describes the proliferation of low-quality, algorithmically generated content — text, images, video, and audio — produced by artificial intelligence systems and released into the information ecosystem with minimal human oversight or editorial judgement. Unlike earlier anxieties about misinformation, which tended to focus on deliberately fabricated content with clear malicious intent, the concern surrounding AI slop is more diffuse: it is not that the content is necessarily false or malicious, but that it is hollow — formulaic, indistinct, and produced at a scale that risks overwhelming the environments in which genuinely valuable human expression once flourished.", "The conditions that gave rise to AI slop are inseparable from the economics of the attention economy. Content platforms — from social media networks to search engines to e-commerce sites — are structurally incentivised to maximise the volume and engagement of content flowing through their systems. [1] For businesses seeking to maintain digital visibility, the appeal of AI-generated content is straightforward: it is fast, cheap, and scalable in ways that human-produced content is not. [2] A single content team that might have produced twenty articles per month can, with AI assistance, publish hundreds — saturating search rankings and social feeds while keeping production costs negligible. [3] The resulting dynamic is one in which the incentives of platforms and the capabilities of AI align to flood information channels with content that is technically coherent but lacks the depth, originality, and authentic perspective that readers, viewers, and listeners actually value. [4]", "The consequences for information quality are considerable. Research published in academic journals examining large language model outputs has documented a tendency toward what linguists describe as \"semantic smoothness\" — a quality in which AI-generated text reads fluently and appears authoritative on the surface, while often failing to contribute genuinely novel insight, acknowledge genuine uncertainty, or reflect the kind of hard-won expertise that emerges from sustained engagement with a subject. In fields where accuracy and nuance matter most — medical journalism, legal commentary, scientific reporting — the substitution of AI-generated summaries for specialist analysis carries risks that go beyond aesthetic dissatisfaction. Patients who encounter AI-generated health content may make decisions based on information that is plausible in register but imprecise in substance.", "The challenge is compounded by the difficulty of discerning AI-generated content from human-produced work, particularly as language models grow more sophisticated. Early AI text was often identifiable by its stilted phrasing, repetitive sentence structure, and tendency to hedge every claim with qualifications. Contemporary models produce prose that many readers find indistinguishable from that of a competent human writer. Detection tools developed to identify AI-generated text have struggled to keep pace with the rapid improvement of generation models, and some researchers have raised concerns that over-reliance on such tools risks incorrectly flagging genuinely human writing — particularly that of non-native speakers whose linguistic patterns may superficially resemble AI output.", "Not all commentators accept the framing of AI-generated content as an unambiguous negative. Some argue that the democratisation of content production enabled by AI tools has genuine value: it allows individuals and small organisations in lower-income countries or underserved communities to participate in the digital information economy from which they were previously excluded by resource constraints. Others suggest that the appropriate response is not to resist AI content production wholesale but to invest in stronger editorial standards, platform accountability mechanisms, and media literacy education that equips audiences to evaluate sources critically. The problem, on this view , is not AI itself but the economic and regulatory structures that currently encourage its deployment in ways that prioritise quantity over quality.", "There is also a longer historical context worth considering. Anxieties about mechanical reproduction degrading cultural quality are not new — they accompanied the invention of the printing press, the rise of mass-market paperback publishing, and the proliferation of cable television. In each case, the predicted collapse of quality did not fully materialise; instead, new hierarchies of value emerged alongside new mechanisms for distinguishing the worthwhile from the disposable. Whether the same equilibrium will reassert itself in the age of generative AI, or whether the scale and speed of AI content production represent a genuinely unprecedented disruption, remains an open question that scholars across disciplines are only beginning to grapple with.", "What the phenomenon of AI slop does make clear is that the digital information environment is not self-correcting. Left unregulated, market incentives alone will not reliably produce conditions in which quality content survives and reaches the audiences that need it. Whether the response comes from platform regulation, professional editorial standards, or a cultural recalibration in which audiences actively seek out and reward authentic human expression, the stakes — for journalism, for education, for public discourse, and for the epistemic foundations of democratic society — are high enough that the question cannot be deferred indefinitely."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central concern of the passage?", options: ["The proliferation of low-quality AI-generated content threatens information quality and public discourse, though the full consequences remain contested.", "Artificial intelligence poses an immediate existential threat to all forms of human creative expression.", "AI language models are incapable of producing content that is useful or accurate in any professional context.", "Social media platforms are primarily responsible for spreading misinformation and should be subject to strict government censorship."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do businesses find AI-generated content particularly appealing for maintaining digital visibility?", options: ["It can be produced at much greater speed and scale than human content while keeping costs minimal.", "Regulatory bodies in most countries currently require companies to disclose human-produced content but not AI-generated material.", "Major search engines actively reward websites that publish AI-generated articles with higher ranking positions.", "AI content consistently receives higher engagement ratings from audiences than human-produced writing."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"discerning\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["reporting or publicly disclosing information", "reading critically and with careful attention", "producing or generating at high volume", "distinguishing one thing from another"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what specific risk does AI-generated health content pose to patients?", options: ["Patients may make health decisions based on content that appears credible but lacks precision.", "It frequently reproduces outdated clinical guidelines that contradict current medical consensus.", "It may expose patients to targeted advertising for pharmaceutical products.", "It tends to recommend alternative medicine over evidence-based treatments."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The phrase \"this view\" in paragraph 5 refers to the perspective that _______.", options: ["AI-generated content is fundamentally indistinguishable from high-quality human writing.", "the appropriate response to AI content is stronger editorial standards and media literacy rather than blanket resistance.", "lower-income countries benefit most from the democratisation of content production through AI tools.", "platform algorithms are intentionally designed to suppress authentic human creative expression."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what problem do current AI detection tools face?", options: ["They can only identify AI-generated images and audio but are unable to analyse written text.", "They are too expensive for most editorial teams and academic institutions to deploy effectively.", "They have failed to keep pace with improvements in AI generation models and risk misidentifying human writing.", "They require access to the original training data of each AI model in order to function accurately."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"grapple\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["to reject or dismiss an argument without consideration", "to struggle seriously with a difficult problem or question", "to summarise or condense a complex argument", "to publish findings in a peer-reviewed academic journal"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what historical parallel does the author draw with AI slop?", options: ["The rise of the internet in the 1990s, which was predicted to destroy academic publishing but instead accelerated the spread of research.", "The invention of photography, which was feared to make traditional painting obsolete but ultimately created new art forms.", "Inventions such as the printing press, mass-market publishing, and cable television also prompted fears about declining cultural quality that did not fully materialise.", "The introduction of radio broadcasting, which initially threatened the newspaper industry before both coexisted successfully."], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This volumetric logic fundamentally reshapes editorial decision-making, subordinating questions of accuracy and originality to the demands of algorithmic performance.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT presented as a counter-argument to the view that AI-generated content is uniformly harmful?", options: ["All AI-generated content should be removed from major platforms to protect journalistic integrity.", "AI tools allow individuals in lower-income countries to participate in the digital information economy.", "Investing in editorial standards and media literacy may be a more effective response than resisting AI entirely.", "The problem lies in economic and regulatory structures rather than in AI technology itself."], answer: 0 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-076", num: 76, title: "Đề 076", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Growing Danger of Wildfires", paragraphs: ["Wildfires have always been a natural part of many of the world's ecosystems, playing an important role in clearing dead vegetation, returning nutrients to the soil, and creating space for new plant growth. For thousands of years, fires set by lightning strikes or volcanic activity have shaped the landscapes of regions such as the Australian bush, the North American forests, and the savannahs of sub-Saharan Africa. However, in recent decades, wildfires have become significantly more frequent, more intense, and more destructive than at any point in recorded history, posing a growing threat to human communities, wildlife, and the global environment.", "One of the main reasons for the increasing danger of wildfires is climate change. Rising global temperatures are causing longer and more severe droughts in many parts of the world, drying out vegetation and making it far easier for fires to start and spread. When plants and grasses are dry, they act like fuel, and even a small spark from a power line, a discarded cigarette, or a lightning strike can ignite a fire that spreads rapidly across hundreds or even thousands of hectares. In Australia, the devastating bushfire season of 2019 to 2020 — widely known as the \"Black Summer\" — burned more than eighteen million hectares of land, destroyed over 3,000 homes, and killed an estimated one billion animals, making it one of the most catastrophic fire events in the country's history.", "Human activity plays a significant role in both starting and worsening wildfires. Studies suggest that approximately eighty-five percent of wildfires in the United States are caused by people, whether through accidental ignition, arson, or the unintentional spread of controlled burns that escape their intended boundaries. Urban expansion into forested and semi-forested areas — a trend known as the wildland-urban interface — places more homes and communities in the path of potential fires and makes it harder for firefighters to contain blazes effectively. At the same time, decades of fire suppression policies in some countries have allowed large quantities of dry undergrowth to accumulate in forests, creating a dense layer of fuel that makes fires far more intense when they do eventually break out.", "The consequences of major wildfires are wide-ranging and long-lasting. In the immediate aftermath of a fire, communities may lose their homes, businesses, and essential infrastructure. Thick smoke from large fires poses serious health risks, particularly for elderly people, children, and those with respiratory conditions such as asthma. After the flames have passed, the destruction of tree roots and ground cover leaves the land vulnerable to soil erosion, flooding, and landslides, particularly when heavy rainfall follows a fire season. Wildlife habitats are severely damaged, and many animal species that depend on specific forest ecosystems struggle to survive or recover after a large fire has swept through their territory.", "Firefighting agencies around the world have developed a range of strategies to prevent and manage wildfires. These include the creation of firebreaks — cleared strips of land that remove vegetation and create a barrier to the spread of fire — as well as the use of aircraft to drop water or fire retardant chemicals on blazes that cannot be safely approached on the ground. In areas where wildfires are a regular seasonal occurrence , authorities carry out planned burns during cooler months to reduce the amount of dry fuel in forests before the dangerous fire season begins. Communities in fire-prone areas are increasingly being trained in evacuation procedures, and early warning systems using satellites and remote sensors are helping emergency services to detect new fires more quickly than ever before.", "Despite these efforts, experts warn that the scale and frequency of wildfires will continue to grow unless the underlying problem of climate change is meaningfully addressed. Without significant reductions in greenhouse gas emissions, rising temperatures will make drought conditions more severe, fire seasons longer, and the areas at risk of catastrophic fire larger with each passing decade. For many communities living in fire-prone regions, learning to live with a greater risk of fire while working toward longer-term solutions has become an unavoidable part of modern life."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main topic of the passage?", options: ["The role of climate change in making wildfires worse and the need for international cooperation to reduce emissions.", "A comparison of firefighting techniques used in different countries to control large-scale wildfires.", "The history of how wildfires have shaped natural ecosystems over thousands of years.", "The growing danger of wildfires, including their causes, consequences, and the measures being taken to address them."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["The Australian bushfire season of 2019 to 2020, known as the Black Summer.", "The number of homes that were destroyed during the Black Summer fires.", "The drought that caused vegetation to dry out across Australia in 2019.", "The estimated one billion animals that were killed during the fires."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how much land did the Australian Black Summer fires burn?", options: ["More than eighteen million hectares, destroying over 3,000 homes.", "Approximately twelve million hectares across five different states.", "Over five million hectares, destroying more than 1,000 homes.", "Nearly thirty million hectares, making it the largest fire in global history."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, approximately what percentage of wildfires in the United States are caused by human activity?", options: ["Approximately seventy percent.", "Around fifty percent.", "Nearly one hundred percent.", "About eighty-five percent."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the purpose of carrying out planned burns during cooler months?", options: ["To clear land for agricultural use in areas where forests have become too dense to manage.", "To reduce the amount of dry fuel in forests before the dangerous fire season starts.", "To create firebreaks by burning strips of land along the edges of communities and roads.", "To practice firefighting techniques and test new equipment before the main fire season begins."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is mentioned as a consequence of wildfires after the flames have passed?", options: ["Communities must abandon their land permanently because the soil becomes too acidic for crops to grow.", "The destruction of tree roots and ground cover leaves land vulnerable to soil erosion, flooding, and landslides.", "Large quantities of carbon dioxide released by the fire accelerate global warming in the surrounding region.", "Rivers and streams become permanently contaminated by the ash and chemicals released during the fire."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a strategy used to prevent or manage wildfires?", options: ["Creating firebreaks by clearing strips of vegetation.", "Building underground shelters where residents can take refuge during fires.", "Training communities in evacuation procedures.", "Using aircraft to drop water or fire retardant chemicals on fires."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"controlled\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Started by arsonists who later attempted to conceal their actions.", "Monitored by scientists to study their effect on the environment.", "Managed and set within deliberate limits for a specific purpose.", "Started accidentally and difficult to stop once they have spread."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"occurrence\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["A problem that can be solved with enough planning.", "A sudden and unexpected natural disaster.", "A natural process that benefits the environment.", "An event or something that happens regularly."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's main purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To inform readers about why wildfires are becoming more dangerous and what is being done to address the problem.", "To persuade readers to move away from fire-prone regions before the situation becomes even more severe.", "To argue that governments are not doing enough to protect communities from the danger of wildfires.", "To describe the history of wildfires around the world and explain why they are necessary for healthy ecosystems."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Effects of Global Warming on People, Nature, and the Planet", paragraphs: ["Global warming — the gradual rise in the Earth's average surface temperature caused primarily by the increasing concentration of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere — is widely recognized as one of the most serious and far-reaching environmental challenges of the twenty-first century. Since the Industrial Revolution began in the mid-eighteenth century, human activities including the burning of fossil fuels, large-scale deforestation, and industrial agriculture have released vast quantities of carbon dioxide, methane, and other gases that trap heat within the Earth's atmosphere. According to data from the World Meteorological Organization, the decade from 2011 to 2020 was the warmest on record globally, and average global temperatures have now risen by approximately 1.1 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels.", "The most direct and widely documented effect of global warming is the accelerating melting of polar ice and glaciers. The Arctic is warming at roughly twice the rate of the global average, causing sea ice to shrink dramatically in both area and thickness. In Greenland and Antarctica, glacial melt has accelerated significantly over the past three decades, contributing to a rise in global sea levels that currently stands at around 3.7 millimeters per year. Scientists project that if current emission trends continue, global sea levels could rise by between 0.3 and one meter by the end of this century, threatening coastal cities including Miami, Jakarta, Shanghai, and Ho Chi Minh City with increased flooding, saltwater intrusion into freshwater supplies, and in the worst cases, permanent inundation .", "Extreme weather events have become more frequent and more severe as global temperatures have risen. Heatwaves that would previously have been considered rare or exceptional are now occurring with much greater regularity across Europe, Asia, and North America. Tropical storms and hurricanes are intensifying more rapidly and producing heavier rainfall than in the past, partly because warmer ocean surface temperatures provide more energy to fuel storm systems. Droughts and wildfires have become longer and more destructive in regions including the Mediterranean, sub-Saharan Africa, and the western United States. The year 2023 was the hottest year ever recorded globally, with average temperatures briefly exceeding 1.5 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels on several occasions — a threshold that scientists and policymakers have identified as a critical boundary for managing climate risks.", "Global warming poses severe threats to biodiversity and natural ecosystems. As temperatures rise and precipitation patterns shift, many plant and animal species are being forced to move to higher altitudes or higher latitudes in search of suitable habitat conditions. Species that cannot migrate quickly enough, or whose habitats are constrained by geography — island species, polar species, or those dependent on specific mountain ecosystems — face an elevated risk of extinction. Coral reefs, which support an estimated twenty-five percent of all marine species despite covering less than one percent of the ocean floor, are particularly vulnerable: ocean warming causes bleaching events in which corals expel the algae they depend on for nutrition, and repeated or prolonged bleaching leads to mass coral death. The Great Barrier Reef in Australia has experienced several mass bleaching events since the late 1990s, with scientists warning that its long-term survival under continued warming scenarios is uncertain.", "The human consequences of global warming are most acutely felt by the populations that have contributed least to the problem. Low-lying island nations such as Tuvalu, Kiribati, and the Maldives face an existential threat from rising sea levels that may render their territories uninhabitable within this century. Agricultural communities in South Asia and sub-Saharan Africa, where rain-fed farming is the primary source of food and income, are increasingly exposed to the disruption of seasonal rainfall patterns, longer droughts, and more unpredictable growing conditions. The World Bank has estimated that climate change could force more than 216 million people to migrate internally within their own countries by 2050, as regions that currently support large populations become too hot, dry, or flood-prone for habitation or agriculture.", "International efforts to address global warming have centered on the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change and its Paris Agreement, adopted in 2015, which committed signatory nations to limiting global warming to well below 2 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels, with efforts to limit warming to 1.5 degrees. Progress has been uneven: while renewable energy capacity has grown dramatically and the cost of solar and wind power has fallen sharply, global greenhouse gas emissions continued to rise until recently and remain far above the levels required to meet the Paris targets. The gap between commitments and actions remains substantial , and scientists warn that the window for avoiding the most severe consequences of warming is narrowing with each year of insufficient action.", "Understanding global warming and its effects is no longer simply a matter for scientists and policymakers. Every individual, community, and organization has a stake in how the world responds — and a role to play in reducing the emissions that drive it. The choices made in the next decade will largely determine the scale of the consequences that future generations inherit."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["A detailed explanation of the scientific causes of global warming and how greenhouse gases trap heat.", "A comparison of the Paris Agreement's targets with the actual emission reductions achieved by signatory countries.", "An argument that wealthy nations should pay developing countries to reduce their greenhouse gas emissions.", "The wide-ranging effects of global warming on weather, ecosystems, human populations, and international policy responses."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, by how much have average global temperatures risen above pre-industrial levels?", options: ["By approximately 0.5 degrees Celsius.", "By nearly 2 degrees Celsius.", "By exactly 1.5 degrees Celsius.", "By around 1.1 degrees Celsius."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, by how much could global sea levels rise by the end of this century if current emission trends continue?", options: ["Between 0.3 and one meter.", "Between five and ten centimeters.", "More than two meters in the worst-case scenario.", "Approximately twenty centimeters."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was significant about the year 2023 in terms of global temperature records?", options: ["It was the hottest year ever recorded globally, with temperatures briefly exceeding 1.5 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels.", "It was the year in which the most severe hurricane season ever recorded caused widespread destruction across three continents.", "It marked the point at which global greenhouse gas emissions first began to decline due to the expansion of renewable energy.", "It was the first year in which global average temperatures exceeded the 2-degree Paris Agreement target."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why are coral reefs particularly vulnerable to global warming?", options: ["Ocean warming causes bleaching events in which corals expel the algae they depend on for nutrition, and repeated bleaching leads to mass coral death.", "They are frequently damaged by the stronger waves and storm surges caused by more powerful tropical cyclones.", "Warming oceans reduce the oxygen levels in shallow water, suffocating the fish and other marine creatures that live on reefs.", "Rising sea levels cause reefs to sink below the depth at which sunlight can penetrate, preventing them from photosynthesizing."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following island nations is NOT mentioned as facing an existential threat from rising sea levels?", options: ["Tuvalu.", "Fiji.", "The Maldives.", "Kiribati."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"trap\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Absorb from the surface and release slowly over time.", "Reflect back into space before it can warm the surface of the Earth.", "Distribute evenly across all layers of the atmosphere.", "Capture and prevent from escaping."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"migrate\" in paragraph 4 refers to the behavior of species that are _______.", options: ["Changing their diet and feeding habits in response to reductions in their natural food sources.", "Moving to different geographic areas to find more suitable living conditions as temperatures rise.", "Adapting their physical characteristics over generations to survive in warmer environments.", "Reproducing at faster rates to compensate for losses caused by rising temperatures."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"substantial\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Recent and the result of policy changes made in the past few years.", "Large and significant in size or degree.", "Technical and difficult for ordinary people to understand without expert guidance.", "Temporary and likely to narrow as more countries ratify the Paris Agreement."], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the fairness of how global warming affects different countries?", options: ["The passage implies that all countries are affected equally by global warming and share an equal responsibility to reduce emissions.", "The passage suggests that wealthier nations are more severely affected than poorer ones because they have more infrastructure and property at risk.", "The passage implies an injustice: the populations most severely threatened by global warming are often those that have contributed least to causing it.", "The passage implies that island nations and agricultural communities have the greatest ability to adapt because they are accustomed to changing conditions."], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Impact of Plastic Pollution on the World's Oceans", paragraphs: ["Since the large-scale commercialization of plastic production began in the 1950s, humanity has manufactured an estimated 9.1 billion metric tons of plastic, of which only approximately nine percent has been recycled. The remainder has been incinerated, deposited in landfills, or — in proportions that researchers are still working to quantify accurately — released into the natural environment. The oceans have become the ultimate destination for a substantial share of this environmental plastic: current estimates suggest that between eight and twelve million metric tons of plastic enter the world's oceans every year, a volume that, if unaddressed, is projected to outweigh all the fish in the sea by 2050. [1]", "Marine plastic pollution takes several distinct forms, each presenting different challenges for both ecosystems and cleanup efforts. Macroplastics — large items such as discarded fishing nets, plastic bottles, bags, and packaging materials — are the most visible component of ocean pollution and the most immediately harmful to large marine animals such as sea turtles, dolphins, whales, and seabirds, which become entangled in nets or mistake plastic debris for food. Ingesting plastic can cause internal injuries, intestinal blockages, and starvation, as animals that have consumed plastic feel falsely full while receiving no nutritional value. One study found that ninety percent of seabirds sampled globally had ingested plastic, compared to fewer than five percent in 1960.", "Microplastics — fragments smaller than five millimeters in diameter — present a different and in some ways more insidious challenge. They enter the marine environment through the fragmentation of larger plastic items under ultraviolet radiation and physical abrasion, and through direct discharge of microbeads from cosmetic and cleaning products and synthetic fibers shed during the washing of polyester clothing. Because of their small size and near-ubiquitous distribution, microplastics are extraordinarily difficult to remove once they have entered the water column. They have been detected in the deep ocean trenches of the Pacific, in Arctic sea ice, in the tissue of commercial fish species sold in supermarkets, and in the blood and placental tissue of humans. [2] The long-term health implications of chronic human exposure to microplastics remain an active area of toxicological research, with preliminary findings suggesting potential disruption of endocrine function, inflammation pathways, and cellular metabolism.", "The geography of ocean plastic accumulation is shaped by the circulation patterns of ocean currents, which concentrate floating debris into large rotating systems known as gyres. The most extensively studied of these is the Great Pacific Garbage Patch — a region of elevated plastic density located between Hawaii and California that is frequently described in popular media as an island of floating trash. This characterization is somewhat misleading : the patch is not a solid mass but a diffuse soup of microplastic particles and fragmented debris that spans an area of roughly 1.6 million square kilometers but has a depth concentration that renders it largely invisible to the naked eye and difficult to quantify precisely from satellite imagery. [3]", "Cleanup technologies designed to address ocean plastic have attracted considerable investment and public attention, but their practical effectiveness remains constrained by the scale and dispersal of the problem. Ocean cleanup systems that use floating barriers to concentrate and collect surface debris have demonstrated proof of concept in controlled trials but struggle with the sheer surface area involved, the proportion of plastic that has already sunk to the seafloor or been ingested by marine organisms, and the significant energy cost and carbon footprint associated with large-scale at-sea operations. Some marine scientists argue that resources devoted to ocean cleanup would generate greater environmental benefit if redirected toward preventing plastic from entering the ocean in the first place — through investment in waste collection infrastructure in high-leakage countries, extended producer responsibility legislation, and the development of genuinely biodegradable alternatives to the plastic polymers whose persistence in the environment is the root cause of the problem. [4]", "The governance of marine plastic pollution reflects the broader difficulty of managing a global commons. No single nation bears primary responsibility for the plastic that circulates in international waters, and the distribution of cleanup costs and regulatory obligations between producing nations, consuming nations, and the maritime industry has proven deeply contentious. The Global Plastics Treaty, which was under negotiation at the United Nations as of 2024, represents the most ambitious attempt yet to establish a binding international framework for reducing plastic production, improving waste management, and financing the transition to a circular plastics economy. Its prospects for adoption and meaningful implementation remain uncertain, but the scientific consensus on the urgency of action has rarely been clearer.", "What the ocean plastic crisis ultimately demonstrates is that the material choices made by industrial societies — which plastics to produce, how to design for end-of-life, who bears the cost of disposal — are not merely economic or logistical questions but ecological and ethical ones, whose consequences are distributed across species, ecosystems, and generations in ways that market prices have historically failed to capture."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best describes the main focus of the passage?", options: ["A comprehensive examination of the scale, forms, ecological impacts, cleanup challenges, and governance of ocean plastic pollution.", "An argument that ocean cleanup technology is the most cost-effective solution to the marine plastic crisis and should receive more investment.", "A scientific report on the specific health effects of microplastic exposure in humans and animals.", "A criticism of plastic-producing nations for failing to accept financial responsibility for the damage their products cause to the global ocean."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what proportion of seabirds sampled globally had ingested plastic in the study mentioned?", options: ["Ninety percent, compared to fewer than five percent in 1960.", "Nearly one hundred percent, with no significant difference between 1960 and the present day.", "Approximately fifty percent, compared to around thirty percent in 1960.", "Around seventy-five percent, a figure that has remained stable since the 1980s."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is mentioned as a source through which microplastics enter the marine environment?", options: ["The deliberate dumping of industrial chemical waste at designated offshore disposal sites.", "Synthetic fibers shed during the washing of polyester clothing.", "The degradation of fishing nets made from natural plant-based materials.", "Runoff from agricultural land treated with plastic-based soil conditioners."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, in which of the following has microplastic contamination NOT been detected?", options: ["Human blood and placental tissue.", "Arctic sea ice.", "The deep ocean trenches of the Pacific.", "The upper layers of the stratosphere."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, approximately how large is the area spanned by the Great Pacific Garbage Patch?", options: ["Over 3 million square kilometers, larger than the continental United States.", "Approximately 500,000 square kilometers, roughly the size of Spain.", "Roughly 1.6 million square kilometers.", "Around 800,000 square kilometers, comparable to the area of Egypt."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was the status of the Global Plastics Treaty as of 2024?", options: ["It had been signed but not yet ratified, pending review by national legislatures in key signatory countries.", "It had been fully adopted and ratified by more than one hundred member states of the United Nations.", "It had been rejected by major plastic-producing nations and was unlikely to move forward without significant revision.", "It was under negotiation at the United Nations, representing the most ambitious attempt yet to establish a binding international framework."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"quantify\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["To publicly report and communicate findings to a wider audience.", "To reduce or limit the amount of something through targeted intervention.", "To measure or express something as a precise numerical amount.", "To prevent something from entering the natural environment."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"misleading\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Deliberately exaggerated for political or commercial purposes.", "Giving a false or inaccurate impression of what something is actually like.", "Difficult to understand without specialist scientific knowledge.", "Scientifically outdated and no longer supported by current research."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"entangled\" in paragraph 2 refers to large marine animals that become _______.", options: ["Trapped or caught up in discarded fishing nets and other plastic materials.", "Attracted to plastic debris because it resembles their natural prey.", "Confused by underwater noise pollution generated by plastic waste collection vessels.", "Gradually weakened by the toxic chemicals that leach from plastic items in seawater."], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'For these scientists, the fundamental leverage point lies upstream — in the design of products, the structure of supply chains, and the policy frameworks that determine how much plastic is produced in the first place.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Local Housing Policies and Their Unintended Effects", paragraphs: ["Housing policy occupies a peculiar place in the landscape of public administration: it is among the most politically consequential domains of local governance, touching as it does on the most fundamental aspects of residents' lives — where they can afford to live, how long their commute will be, which schools their children can attend, and how much of their income is consumed by shelter costs — yet it is also among the most reliably productive of unintended consequences. Interventions that appear rational, humane, and technically sound at the moment of their adoption have repeatedly generated outcomes that their architects neither anticipated nor desired. [1] Understanding why housing markets respond to policy interventions in counterintuitive ways is not merely an academic exercise but a practical necessity for any government that hopes to improve housing affordability and access without simultaneously worsening the conditions it set out to remedy.", "Rent control — the practice of setting legal limits on the rent that landlords may charge for residential accommodation — is perhaps the most extensively studied and most persistently controversial of housing policy instruments. Its appeal is intuitive: in cities where housing costs have risen far faster than incomes, limiting rent increases protects existing tenants from displacement and preserves the economic and social diversity of neighborhoods that unregulated market forces might otherwise homogenize . The empirical evidence on rent control's effects, however, is considerably more complicated than its advocates generally acknowledge. A study of San Francisco's rent control ordinance found that while it successfully protected covered tenants from displacement, it simultaneously reduced the overall supply of rental housing in the city by approximately fifteen percent, as landlords converted rental units to condominiums, redeveloped properties, or simply removed them from the rental market to avoid the restrictions — a supply contraction that placed upward pressure on rents for all the units that remained uncontrolled. [2]", "Zoning regulations — the legal rules that govern what types of structures may be built in particular areas of a city — present a second category of housing policy whose unintended consequences have attracted intense scholarly and political attention in recent years. Single-family zoning, which restricts residential development in large portions of many American, Canadian, and Australian cities to detached single-family homes, was originally introduced in the early twentieth century as a tool of urban planning intended to separate residential areas from industrial and commercial uses. Its contemporary critics argue that it has become the primary structural cause of housing unaffordability in many high-demand cities, by legally prohibiting the construction of the denser, more affordable housing types — apartments, townhouses, accessory dwelling units — that market demand and demographic change would otherwise produce. [3] Several cities, including Minneapolis, Auckland, and Portland, Oregon, have eliminated single-family-only zoning across all or most of their territories, and early evidence from these experiments is being watched closely by urban planners worldwide.", "Inclusionary zoning — a policy requiring developers of new market-rate housing to include a specified proportion of units affordable to lower-income households — represents an attempt to use the development process itself as a mechanism for generating affordable housing without direct public expenditure. Its attractiveness to fiscally constrained local governments is evident: it appears to deliver a social benefit at no cost to the public purse. The economic reality, however, is more nuanced. When affordable housing requirements impose costs on developers that cannot be offset by the revenues from market-rate units, the rational response is to reduce the total volume of housing produced — a dynamic that has been documented in cities including New York, London, and Sydney, where high inclusionary requirements have been cited by developers as a contributing factor in decisions to scale back or abandon projects entirely. The result, paradoxically, can be less total housing and less affordable housing than would have been produced under a less demanding inclusionary regime or under a direct subsidy model funded by general taxation.", "The difficulty of designing effective housing policy is compounded by the political economy of the housing market. [4] Existing homeowners — who in most high-income democracies represent the majority of adult voters — have a direct financial interest in the appreciation of property values, which is generally enhanced by restricting the supply of new housing. Renters and prospective first-time buyers, whose interests lie in increased supply and lower prices, are typically less organized, less politically influential, and less likely to vote in local elections where housing decisions are primarily made. This systematic misalignment between political power and the public interest in housing affordability helps explain why housing supply restrictions have proven so durable in the face of evidence of their harmful consequences: they are not policy failures in the ordinary sense but policy outcomes that serve the interests of the most politically powerful constituents.", "Several researchers have proposed that the most reliable path toward genuinely affordable housing lies not in any single policy instrument but in the combination of supply expansion, targeted demand-side subsidies, and institutional reforms that reduce the capacity of incumbent property owners to block densification. Land value taxes — which shift the tax burden from improvements (buildings) toward the unimproved land value beneath them — have been proposed as a mechanism for simultaneously encouraging more intensive land use and reducing the speculative incentive to hold land undeveloped while waiting for values to rise. Whether political systems structured around the preferences of property-owning majorities are capable of adopting such reforms at the scale required remains, in most jurisdictions, an open question whose answer has more to do with political will than with the state of the evidence.", "What housing policy ultimately reveals is a recurrent tension in democratic governance between short-term local interests and long-term collective welfare — a tension that is especially acute in housing because the benefits of increased supply are diffuse and future-oriented while the costs, in the form of neighborhood change and property value risk, are concentrated and immediate. Navigating that tension requires not only better policy design but a more honest public conversation about whose interests current arrangements serve and whose they do not."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best captures the central argument of the passage?", options: ["The only effective solution to housing unaffordability is large-scale public housing construction funded directly by national governments.", "Local housing policies consistently fail because local governments lack the technical expertise to design effective interventions.", "Rent control is the most harmful housing policy instrument and should be abolished in all cities where it currently operates.", "Housing policies that appear rational and humane often generate counterintuitive unintended consequences, and understanding why requires examining both economic mechanisms and the political interests that shape policy design."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did a study of San Francisco's rent control ordinance find regarding housing supply?", options: ["Rent control caused a thirty percent increase in condominium conversions, which fully offset the reduction in rental unit supply.", "Rent control reduced the overall supply of rental housing in the city by approximately fifteen percent as landlords removed units from the rental market.", "Rent control increased the overall supply of rental housing by encouraging new construction of purpose-built rental units.", "Rent control had no measurable effect on housing supply but significantly reduced tenant turnover in covered units."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following housing types is NOT mentioned as being prohibited by single-family zoning?", options: ["Apartments.", "Accessory dwelling units.", "Mixed-use commercial and residential towers.", "Townhouses."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which cities are mentioned as having eliminated single-family-only zoning across all or most of their territories?", options: ["Stockholm, Singapore, and Toronto.", "San Francisco, Vancouver, and Melbourne.", "Minneapolis, Auckland, and Portland, Oregon.", "London, Berlin, and Tokyo."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do land value taxes aim to encourage more intensive land use?", options: ["By providing tax rebates to developers who build affordable housing units as part of mixed-income developments.", "By imposing higher taxes on vacant properties in high-demand urban areas to compel their sale to developers.", "By exempting social housing providers from all property-related taxes in exchange for setting below-market rents.", "By shifting the tax burden from buildings toward unimproved land value, reducing the incentive to hold land undeveloped while waiting for values to rise."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"homogenize\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Upgrade and improve the quality of housing throughout a neighborhood.", "Make uniform and remove variety by replacing different elements with similar ones.", "Rapidly increase in value in a way that benefits only existing property owners.", "Divide and separate into distinct zones based on income level or land use."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"Its\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["The fiscal situation of local governments that cannot afford direct housing subsidies.", "Inclusionary zoning as a housing policy instrument.", "The general principle of using development processes to generate public benefits.", "The affordability requirement imposed on developers of new market-rate housing."], answer: 1 }, { q: "What does the passage imply about the relationship between inclusionary zoning and total housing supply?", options: ["Demanding inclusionary requirements can paradoxically reduce total housing production if the affordable unit costs cannot be offset by revenues from market-rate units.", "High inclusionary requirements reliably increase the total volume of housing produced because they attract socially motivated developers.", "Inclusionary requirements have no measurable effect on developer decisions because profit margins in housing development are too large to be affected.", "Inclusionary zoning is most effective when combined with rent control, as the two policies address complementary aspects of housing unaffordability."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's view of the political obstacles to housing reform?", options: ["The author suggests that national governments should override local zoning decisions to force densification in high-demand cities.", "The author implies that housing supply restrictions persist not because governments lack knowledge but because they serve the financial interests of property-owning majorities who dominate local political processes.", "The author believes that technical disagreements among economists about the best housing policy are the primary obstacle to reform.", "The author argues that renters and first-time buyers simply do not care enough about housing policy to vote in sufficient numbers to change it."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Through their participation in local planning processes, neighborhood associations, and city council elections, existing homeowners have historically exercised influence over zoning and development decisions that far exceeds their numerical share of the population.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 3 }] },
  ] },
];
